Definitions:
- J^-(q) = {p ∈ M : p ≤ q, p causally precedes q} (causal past, including null).
- Tail predicate T(γ): ∃q ∈ I+, ∃t0 ∈ [0,T): γ([t0,T)) ⊆ J^-(q) ∩ M.
- Set predicate S(γ): γ([0,T)) ⊆ ⋃_{q∈I+} J^-(q) ∩ M = J^-(I+) ∩ M.

Claim (F1 rev13): T(γ) ⇒ S(γ). Proof: if γ([t0,T)) ⊆ J^-(q), then for t ≥ t0, γ(t) ∈ J^-(q) ⊆ J^-(I+). For t < t0, since γ is future-directed causal and M is chronological/causal, γ(t) ≤ γ(t0) (there's a causal curve from γ(t) to γ(t0)). γ(t0) ∈ J^-(q), and J^-(q) is past-closed: p ≤ γ(t0) ≤ q ⇒ p ∈ J^-(q). So γ(t) ∈ J^-(q) ⊆ J^-(I+). Hence whole curve in J^-(I+). ✓ T ⇒ S.

Converse S(γ) ⇒ T(γ)? γ([0,T)) ⊆ J^-(I+) means each γ(t) is in some J^-(q_t), with q_t possibly varying and no single q capturing the tail. In general not equivalent (the T4 omega-chain separates them). So S strictly weaker predicate than T. Therefore F1 rev13's "variant SET predicate is strictly WEAKER than the class predicate" is correct, and F0's "the set-based reading ... is strictly stronger" is wrong at the predicate level. ✓ My analysis holds. Note: at the statement level ("no S-visible singularity" vs "no T-visible singularity"), the S-negation is strictly stronger (because S ⊇ T). So F0's sentence is at best ambiguous, at worst wrong; F1's is precise. Good: the finding is that F0's wording is imprecise/inverted relative to F1.

